I found two genuine planning gaps that should become explicit blockers rather than hidden assumptions: the legacy SOCKS product’s migration mode, and the launch localization/copy-approval policy. I’ll model both as startable decision tasks that block only their downstream implementation; no story will be marked `blocked`.
exec
/bin/zsh -lc "task-board m 'set_status(STORY-260715-tx1tbz, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-tx1tbz, title=\"Cross-platform profile and private-key management\", description=\"Deliver production profile CRUD and SSH public-key credential management for the iOS and macOS containing apps while preserving a least-privilege app-to-extension boundary. The containing apps own user edits; packet-tunnel providers consume immutable non-secret profile generations and resolve credentials only through opaque Keychain references.\", scope=\"In scope: profile list, create, edit, validation, selection, deletion, display name, canonical host or address, SSH port, account, private-key selection, optional passphrase handling, key import, key generation, explicit export, App Group publication, Data Protection Keychain storage, shared app/extension access-group boundaries, iOS and macOS presentation, accessibility identifiers, unit/integration/UI tests, and cleanup semantics. Out of scope: password-only SSH, agent forwarding, arbitrary shell sessions, OpenSSH config import, ProxyJump, public SOCKS configuration, host-trust confirmation UI, live VPN controls, and legacy SOCKS migration policy.\", ac=\"1. On iOS and macOS a user can create, validate, edit, select, and delete profiles containing display name, canonical hostname or address, SSH port, account, and a private-key reference, with field-specific accessible errors. 2. App Group storage contains only versioned non-secret profile snapshots and opaque Keychain references, is published atomically, and never contains raw keys or passphrases. 3. Imported and generated keys use the approved formats and platform cryptography, live in the minimum Data Protection Keychain access group, and are never exported without an explicit user action. 4. Deletion and replacement preserve referential integrity, handle active-profile conflicts explicitly, and remove secrets only when no retained profile references them. 5. The M1 profile loader and extension Keychain resolver consume a published generation without secrets in providerConfiguration, logs, diagnostics, or board artifacts. 6. Swift tests plus iOS and macOS UI tests cover valid and invalid profiles, import, generation, protected keys, selection, deletion, relaunch persistence, accessibility, and screenshot review.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-14qi1v, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-14qi1v, title=\"Mandatory host trust and authentication recovery experience\", description=\"Expose mandatory SSH host verification and public-key authentication as explicit, comprehensible user journeys on iOS and macOS. First use requires an informed trust decision; a changed host key stops safe connection and requires a separate inspected replacement action.\", scope=\"In scope: canonical host identity records, SHA-256 fingerprints, algorithms, provenance, first-seen and last-seen metadata, first-use trust request, explicit approval, changed-key high-severity blocking state, inspected replacement or cancellation, revocation, passphrase access, authentication and Keychain error remediation, session-level multi-lane identity consistency, privacy-safe error copy, accessibility, and UI/security tests. Out of scope: silent host acceptance, StrictHostKeyChecking=no behavior, password-only authentication, agent forwarding, SSH engine implementation, general profile editing, packet forwarding, and automatic trust replacement.\", ac=\"1. Authentication cannot succeed before an approved host identity is matched or the user explicitly approves a first-use fingerprint showing the complete SHA-256 value and algorithm. 2. A changed, revoked, or inconsistent lane identity stops connection before user authentication, presents a high-severity non-dismissive explanation, and cannot reconnect or auto-accept until the user inspects and explicitly replaces trust. 3. Approved records retain canonical host, algorithm, fingerprint, provenance, and first/last-seen timestamps without storing secrets. 4. Passphrase, missing-key, inaccessible-Keychain, rejected-key, and unsupported-algorithm failures map to distinct privacy-safe remediation with no credential echo. 5. Every SSH lane in one session is evaluated against the same approved identity generation and an identity disagreement produces one terminal session error. 6. Unit, integration, and iOS/macOS UI tests cover first use, cancel, repeat connection, changed key, replacement, revocation, auth rejection, protected keys, lane mismatch, accessibility, and screenshot review.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-309t4z, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-309t4z, title=\"Truthful VPN connection controls, capability status, and policy settings\", description=\"Provide native iOS and macOS controls that start and stop the owned Network Extension session and explain its authoritative lifecycle and forwarding capability without making the containing app part of the live tunnel.\", scope=\"In scope: selected-profile controls, connect, disconnect, system VPN permission, owned-manager errors, disconnected, connecting, connected/full, connected/degraded, reasserting, disconnecting, and failed presentation, capability and reason details, profile switching, QUIC Auto/Allow/Block UDP 443 policy, compatible and platform-scoped fail-closed routing choices, stale-state handling, iOS and macOS presentation, accessibility, automated UI tests, and physical system-indicator checks. Out of scope: packet/SSH/relay implementation, redefining capability predicates, release signing, TestFlight, App Review submission, analytics, and legacy public SOCKS controls.\", ac=\"1. iOS and macOS users can select an eligible profile, grant required system VPN permission, connect, disconnect, and recover after containing-app relaunch while the Network Extension session remains authoritative. 2. The UI renders exactly the required lifecycle states and never reports connected/full unless the current provider generation confirms TCP, leak-safe DNS, UDP, and required address-family capability. 3. Degraded mode remains connected, names the missing UDP capability and safe limitations, while failed is reserved for a mandatory safe-forwarding failure. 4. Reasserting, disconnecting, stale snapshots, permission denial, configuration failure, host trust/auth failure, and tunnel setup failure have deterministic controls and accessible remediation. 5. QUIC and routing settings expose only policies supported by the accepted M3 contracts, explain compatible versus fail-closed limitations, and apply through versioned profile generations. 6. Deterministic state tests, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical-device checks verify controls, system VPN indicator, host-app termination continuity, full/degraded transitions, profile switching, accessibility, and screenshots.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-3tds7d, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-3tds7d, title=\"Privacy-safe diagnostics, disclosure, and support export\", description=\"Give users useful runtime and support information without exposing credentials, traffic, destinations, DNS queries, or full local addresses. Disclose the baseline VPN data flow and zero-telemetry posture before enablement and provide an inspectable, redacted, user-initiated export.\", scope=\"In scope: bounded runtime events and aggregate metrics, versioned diagnostic snapshots, approved and prohibited fields, redaction, retention and deletion behavior, diagnostics summary UI, support-export category preview, manifest, platform share/save handoff, pre-VPN privacy disclosure, settings disclosure, zero analytics and traffic telemetry verification, exit-host and system-exclusion explanations, accessibility, unit/security/UI tests, and physical acceptance. Out of scope: packet payload capture, DNS or destination logging, raw addresses, crash-report vendor integration, opt-out analytics for baseline, future telemetry, public privacy-policy hosting, legal licensing decisions, and release submission.\", ac=\"1. Default diagnostics are bounded and limited to approved timestamps, states, lane identifiers, aggregate counts, error domains/codes, memory observations, negotiated algorithms, relay identity, and redacted address-family facts. 2. Automated adversarial tests prove keys, passphrases, packet payloads, DNS names, destination hosts or IPs, full local addresses, shell input, and traffic samples cannot enter logs, snapshots, crash annotations, or support exports. 3. A user can inspect diagnostic categories, preview the exact export manifest, create an export only on explicit action, and delete retained local support data on iOS and macOS. 4. Before first VPN enablement the app explains processed data, the user-controlled SSH exit, exit-host visibility, Relux Works absence from the baseline data path, system-excluded traffic, retention/deletion, and zero traffic telemetry. 5. Extension-to-app diagnostic transfer is versioned, size-bounded, stale-generation safe, and remains useful after containing-app relaunch. 6. Unit, property/fuzz where appropriate, UI, and physical acceptance tests verify redaction, bounds, disclosure gating, export preview/output, deletion, accessibility, and no analytics or traffic-telemetry endpoints.\"); set_status(STORY-260715-n5dt84, status=analysis); set_details(STORY-260715-n5dt84, title=\"Onboarding, accessibility, localization, and legacy migration\", description=\"Guide a new user from product understanding and SSH prerequisites through profile creation, host trust, privacy acknowledgement, system VPN permission, first connection, recovery, and support on iOS and macOS. Establish release-grade accessibility and an explicit migration boundary from the current macOS SOCKS product.\", scope=\"In scope: first-run and resumable onboarding, SSH host prerequisite guidance, privacy acknowledgement before VPN enablement, profile/trust/permission handoffs, empty and recoverable error states, settings/help entry points, VoiceOver and macOS accessibility, keyboard navigation, focus order, Dynamic Type and text scaling, contrast, reduced motion, localization-ready copy, pseudo-localization, shared accessibility identifiers, current SOCKS coexistence/replacement/retirement decision, approved defaults/history migration, iOS/macOS UI tests, and physical end-to-end acceptance. Out of scope: implementing a public SOCKS server, OpenSSH config import, ProxyJump, release notarization, TestFlight upload, App Store submission, regional licensing decisions, and unsupported locale translations.\", ac=\"1. A first-time user on iOS and macOS can understand the self-hosted SSH model, complete or resume prerequisites, create a profile, trust a host, acknowledge privacy terms, grant VPN permission, connect, understand capability or failure, obtain help, and disconnect. 2. Privacy disclosure appears before the first enable action and onboarding does not claim anonymity, a Relux exit network, or capabilities not present in the selected mode. 3. All M4 surfaces have stable shared accessibility identifiers, meaningful labels/hints/values, logical focus, keyboard operation on macOS, VoiceOver operation, supported text scaling, sufficient contrast, and reduced-motion behavior. 4. User-facing strings are externalized, pseudo-localization and text expansion pass, and the approved launch-locale policy and fallback are documented. 5. A reviewed decision selects legacy SOCKS coexistence, replacement, or retirement and defines handling of host/account/port defaults, existing release history, bundle/storage identity, rollback, and user messaging before migration code runs. 6. Automated UI journeys plus named physical iPhone and Apple-silicon Mac acceptance cover first run, relaunch/resume, permission denial, empty/error recovery, migration path, full/degraded/failure states, diagnostics, accessibility, and screenshot review.\")'" in /Users/iv/Developer/relux-proxy
